# Universal Product Excellence

**Intent:** Single owner for product philosophy, cross-surface UI and accessibility (§2a–2c), security, privacy, and Definition of Done — not HLD diagrams, web deploy, game engines, or browser bots.

A generic engineering + design + security rule applicable to any product (static site, CLI, web, mobile, full-stack, embedded), any tech stack, any team. Items are tagged by applicability:

- **[core]** — applies to every product.
- **[applicable if …]** — applies only when the product matches the listed condition (UI, mobile, multi-tenant, etc.). Skip otherwise.

If a tag does not apply to your product, drop the whole bullet — do not water it down.

## 1. Product Philosophy **[core]**

Articulate the **one real user job** in one sentence before writing code. Ruthlessly say *no* to features that do not serve that job. Obsess over first-run experience; make the default path the right path. Prefer **one clear primary action** per screen, predictable affordances, no dark patterns, no jargon. Measure success by *time-to-value* and *task completion rate*, not feature count.

## 1b. Dieter Rams — ten principles (digital products) **[applicable if you ship any UI]**

Industrial design principles (Braun, Vitsœ) apply directly to software UI: clarity over ornament, function over decoration. Use this checklist when reviewing layouts, components, or flows.

| Principle | What to enforce in UI/code |
|-----------|----------------------------|
| **Innovative** | Novel patterns only when they improve the user job; do not innovate for showcase effect. |
| **Useful** | Every control earns its place; remove chrome that does not help completion. |
| **Aesthetic** | Restrained typography and color; calm hierarchy — beauty from order, not noise. |
| **Understandable** | Labels match mental models; icons have text or tooltips; errors say what to do next. |
| **Unobtrusive** | UI defers to content; avoid modal stacks, interruptive banners, and competing CTAs. |
| **Honest** | UI state matches reality (loading, empty, errors); no fake urgency, counts, or disabled faux affordances. |
| **Long-lasting** | Design tokens and shared components over one-off styles; avoid trend skins that date in a year. |
| **Thorough** | Align pixels *and* edge cases — focus rings, truncation, RTL, slow network, small screens. |
| **Environmentally friendly** | Lean assets, lazy load, respect reduced motion and battery; avoid shipping MB of unused CSS/JS. |
| **As little design as possible** | Flat, legible surfaces by default; add decoration only when it teaches or delights without clutter. |

**Skeuomorphism vs flat [applicable if visual UI]:** Use real-world metaphor only for unfamiliar controls; otherwise prefer flat hierarchy (type, spacing, contrast). Decorative skeuomorphism violates *honest* and *as little design as possible*.

**Design responsibility [core with UI]:** Align UI with privacy and ethics (§6); reject patterns that fail *honest* or *unobtrusive*.

## 2a. Cross-Surface Product Principles **[applicable if you ship any UI]**

The same user job must complete on every surface; layout and chrome may differ.

- **Input modalities [applicable if UI]:** support whichever of pointer / touch / keyboard / assistive tech the surface actually receives; visible focus on every interactive element.
  - **[applicable if touch UI]** meet or exceed platform minimum touch targets (~44–48 density-independent units — 44 pt iOS HIG, 48 dp Android Material) with adequate spacing.
  - **[applicable if desktop/web UI]** full keyboard-only path for every primary task.
- **Accessibility [applicable if consumer-facing UI, regulated industry, or public-sector product]:** meet **WCAG 2.2 AA** — logical focus order, accessible name + description for custom controls, sufficient colour contrast, errors announced and linked inline (not modal-only on small screens). Use **`aria-live="polite"`** for inline form errors; **focus trap** in modals with focus restored on close; **skip-to-main** link for keyboard users; **`aria-current`** on active nav items. Internal admin tools may scope to keyboard + screen-reader smoke checks only.
- **Motion & vision [applicable if UI has animations or theming]:** respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; never convey state through motion alone. Honour **system font scaling / Dynamic Type**, high-contrast modes, and `prefers-color-scheme`.
- **Layout beyond width [applicable if UI on phones/tablets]:** mobile-first, container queries where supported, **safe areas / notches**, **RTL** via CSS logical properties / platform RTL APIs. Decide explicit **content vs navigation priority** at narrow widths — truncate, reflow, or "more" pattern.
- **Network reality [applicable if UI hits a network]:** every screen has explicit **loading, empty, error, and partial-data** states that work on slow or flaky links. Skeletons over spinners; retry affordances; never blank-screen on failure.
- **Offline / degraded behaviour [applicable if mobile or field-use product]:** define which flows must work offline; queue mutations with idempotency keys; reconcile on reconnect.

## 2b. Design System Across Surfaces **[applicable if 2+ client surfaces share a brand]**

Share **design tokens** (spacing, type, colour, radius, elevation, motion). Use shared **component semantics** (same labels, roles, behaviour for the same job on every client). Design **APIs that don't assume desktop**: pagination sized for mobile, optional/sparse field sets, attachment-size limits.

## 2c. Internationalization & Localization **[applicable if shipped outside one locale]**

- Externalize all user-visible strings; use ICU MessageFormat-style placeholders for plurals/gender — no concatenation.
- **Locale-aware formats** for dates, numbers, currency, units, addresses, names.
- **RTL** mirroring of layout, icons, and gestures **[applicable if any RTL locale is supported]**.
- **Timezone discipline:** store UTC, render in user's zone; one-off events → UTC instant; scheduled events → wall-clock + IANA zone (DST-safe); recurring events → **RRULE + IANA zone [applicable if calendar/scheduling product]**.
- Pseudo-localize in CI to catch hard-coded strings and truncation early.

## 2d. Nearest-product UI (familiarity) **[applicable if you ship any UI]**

When the product sits in a **known category** or **extends a familiar product**, anchor layout, typography, color, density, and interaction patterns to the **nearest reference users already know** — not a generic AI dashboard or unrelated SaaS template. Users should feel *at home* on first open. This is about **structure and feel**, not copying trademarks (use original assets, icons, and copy; study patterns, spacing, and hierarchy).

**When building with Lovable or similar UI generators:** name the nearest reference product explicitly in the prompt or `product_spec.md` *before* the first generation pass.

| What you are building | Nearest UI / feel to study (examples) |
|-----------------------|----------------------------------------|
| Scheduler or tooling **inside** a messenger ecosystem | That messenger itself (e.g. WhatsApp scheduler → WhatsApp chat list, bubbles, greens, bottom nav) |
| Browser extension for a **specific site** | That site’s panels, typography, and chrome (e.g. LeetCode extension → LeetCode layout, monospace blocks, tab styling) |
| **Tile / merge puzzle** (e.g. Equalize, 2048-family) | Classic minimal board games (e.g. 2048 — grid, tile motion, score strip, restrained palette) |
| **Side-view fighting / brawler** (e.g. Ranbhoomi) | Genre peers (e.g. Little Fighter — roster strip, health bars, compact HUD, arcade density) |
| **Affirmations / wellness copy** | Leading affirmation apps *or* editorial calm from known creators (e.g. Louise Hay–style sites — soft type, generous whitespace, gentle CTAs) |
| **Music / audio playback** | Dominant players in that niche (e.g. Spotify — now playing, queue, library tabs, dark-first chrome) |

If no single product fits, pick the **closest category leader** and document the choice in the spec. For games, also apply `games-building.mdc` §1 (visual direction lock) and keep HUD minimal per §5.

## 3. Architecture Principles

**[core]** Apply **SOLID, DRY, KISS, YAGNI, twelve-factor**, and clean/hexagonal layering. **Structured logs with correlation IDs** for anything that runs. Never trust user IDs from request bodies — derive from the auth token.

**[applicable if the product serves traffic or runs a service]** RED metrics (Rate, Errors, Duration) with p50/p95/p99.

**[applicable if the product makes network calls, has async work, or runs multi-instance]** Idempotent APIs with idempotency keys; retry + exponential backoff + jitter; circuit breakers; timeouts at every hop; DLQs for poison messages; at-least-once delivery with idempotent processing (never claim *exactly-once*).

**[applicable if 2+ services or > 1 deploy environment]** Distributed tracing, SLOs with error budgets, feature flags, canary/blue-green deploys with one-click rollback.

## 4. Testing Discipline

Bar is **behavioural tests at trust boundaries**, not a line-coverage number. Each repo declares its own minimum (e.g., "changed lines covered" or "critical paths covered") in its README/ADR.

- **Unit / component tests [core]:** in whatever framework the language uses. Test behaviour, not implementation.
- **Accessibility automation [applicable if consumer-facing UI or regulated industry]:** axe-core (web), ESLint a11y rules, XCTest accessibility APIs (iOS), Compose UI / Espresso accessibility checks (Android), or equivalent in CI.
- **Integration tests [applicable if you have a DB, queue, or external service]:** real dependencies via Testcontainers/Docker; mock only at the network boundary.
- **Contract tests [applicable if 2+ services talk]:** Pact or OpenAPI schema tests.
- **E2E tests [applicable if you ship a UI with critical multi-step flows]:** Playwright / Cypress / Maestro / XCUITest / Espresso on a real device + browser matrix. Use **test data factories** and parameterized boundary cases; **Page Object** helpers when the same locators appear in 3+ tests.
- **Visual regression [applicable if brand-critical UI or high design churn]:** snapshot only **stable, low-churn chrome** (headers, nav) — not volatile content or gameplay frames.
- **Load tests [applicable if you have SLOs or significant concurrency]:** k6/Locust/JMeter.

CI blocks merge on red **[core]**.

## 5. Security Non-Negotiables

Threat-model every feature.

**[core, every product]**
- **Zero secrets in code/repos** — use a vault; push protection + pre-commit `gitleaks`/`trufflehog`; rotate on schedule and on suspicion.
- **Validate every input at trust boundaries.**
- **SAST + DAST + dependency scanning** in CI; follow OWASP Top 10.
- **Least-privilege IAM**; encrypt at rest and in transit (TLS 1.3); mask sensitive values in logs.

**[applicable only if the listed component exists]**
- **Strict CSP, HSTS, SameSite cookies [applicable if web surface].**
- **Parameterised queries / ORMs [applicable if SQL DB]** — no string-concatenated SQL.
- **Row-Level Security [applicable if multi-tenant DB].**
- **Webhook signing** — HMAC + timestamp + short replay window, constant-time verify **[applicable if you send or receive webhooks]**.
- **Account takeover defences** — MFA, breached-password checks, auth rate-limit + lockout, device fingerprinting, session rotation on privilege change **[applicable if user accounts]**.
- **Supply-chain hardening** — SLSA levels, signed artifacts, SBOMs **[applicable if you publish binaries/images]**.
- **Mobile platform hardening** — certificate pinning, jailbreak/root detection where the threat model demands, keystore/keychain for secrets, no PII in app logs **[applicable if native mobile app]**.

## 6. Privacy & Product Ethics **[applicable if you handle personal data]**

- **Data classification:** label every field as public / internal / PII / regulated, so IAM, encryption, retention, and audit policies line up consistently.
- **Data minimization:** collect only what the user's job requires.
- **Purpose limitation:** use data only for what was disclosed; no silent repurposing.
- **Retention:** define and enforce TTLs; honour GDPR/CCPA delete/export; keep audit trails **[applicable if regulated jurisdiction]**.
- **User-visible honesty:** plain-language explanations of what is collected and why; no dark patterns in consent.

## 7. Definition of Done **[core]**

A change ships only when: it serves one clear user job; the simplest design was chosen; behavioural tests pass; observability is wired for any component running in production; threat model reviewed for applicable risks; docs/runbooks updated; rollback is one click away.

- **[applicable if UI]** verified on smallest + largest supported viewport, keyboard-only, and one representative mobile profile; spot-check against Rams §1b (especially *understandable*, *honest*, *as little design as possible*).
- **[applicable if accessibility scope applies]** reduced-motion and high-contrast spot-checked; screen-reader smoke test on primary flow.
- **[applicable if i18n scope applies]** critical flows spot-checked in a secondary locale, and in RTL if any RTL locale is supported.
- **[applicable if native mobile app]** tested on min-supported OS and current OS; light + dark mode; Dynamic Type / font scaling.

For architecture sign-off before coding, see `system-design.mdc` §4.
